## Tool Priority
1. **LSP first for symbol work** — use the `LSP` tool for go-to-definition, find-references, hover types, rename, and diagnostics. More accurate than Grep for symbols and cheaper than re-reading whole files. Grep only for free text, comments, strings, or languages with no LSP.
2. Built-in tools (Read/Write/Edit/Glob/Grep/Agent/Skills) and MCP servers
3. CLI tools (`gh`, `git`, `docker`, `curl`, `npm`)
4. Manual workarounds last

**Concurrency:** prefer subagents (Agent tool / background tasks) over spawning new panes/windows for parallel or long-running work. Attachable interactive surfaces only when the operator asks.
**Terminal multiplexer is herdr, NOT tmux** — no tmux server runs on this machine. Pane/tab/layout ops and the agent-session lifecycle go through the `herdr` CLI / socket API (`~/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`). Key: `herdr status`, `herdr session list`, `herdr pane list`, `herdr agent list` (JSON — parse it before assuming which sessions run), `herdr agent read <pane_id> --lines N` (recon before touching a pane), `herdr agent start <NAME> --kind <KIND> --pane <ID>` (boots an agent into a pane at its shell prompt — quit the existing session first), `herdr agent send-keys`/`herdr agent prompt` (drive input). Config: `~/.config/herdr/config.toml`. Agent model config lives outside herdr (omp=`~/.omp/agent/config.yml`; Claude=this file + `settings.json`) — restarting the session is how a config change takes effect for that pane. Full quickref in the Estate Brief "Terminal multiplexer — herdr" section (rendered from `agentic/packages/context/estate.md`).
**Agent shape — single-agent default (2026-07-04):** multi-agent generation is a trap — spawned workers lose uncommitted work at turn end, re-spawns start cold, coordination overhead compounds. One long-running Fable/Opus agent (or the main session inline) carries the work end-to-end with commit-as-you-go. Subagent spawns are reserved for read-only recon and mechanical critic/validation gates ("builders fabricate, critics catch" still holds). Multi-agent orchestration only on explicit operator request.

